WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.400
Unions will take a major blow in the upcoming Janus vs. AFSCME case

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.220
if the Supreme Court rules that agency fees are unconstitutional.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.500
Agency fees, which some states require

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.840
non-union employees to pay unions for representation,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.380
have been a lynchpin of union membership. Take the NEA –

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.160
America's largest teachers union. The 22 states with agency fees

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.360
are home to 69% of NEA members.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.780
Agency fees are pivotal because they change the cost of union membership. Take California:

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.100
Teachers can pay a thousand dollars to join the union, or not join

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.400
but still pay six hundred and fifty dollars in agency fees. With no fees,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:49.780
teachers choose to pay a thousand dollars or zero. As the cost of joining soars, union membership will fall.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.880
No one knows by how much, but since 2010, three states dropped agency fees

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.980
and in them, the NEA shrank between 17% and 59%.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.820
A defeat in Janus will weaken America's most powerful

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.620
education interest groups. The long-term question is, "By how much?"

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:13.080
